WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.300
Hi I'm Georgina from BBC Learning English.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.700
In today's lesson,

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.500
we're going to look at 3 differences between

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.060
American English and British English spelling.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.340
Words which end with '–er' in American English,

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.520
often end in '–re' in British English.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:18.420
For example:

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.480
'I live in the center of town.'

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.580
changes to 'centre'.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.920
Normally, verbs which end in '–ize' in American English

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:32.660
can be spelt in British English with either '–ize' or '–ise'.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:33.920
For example:

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.620
'Can you organize the meeting?',

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.899
changes to 'organise' (or 'organize').

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:41.800
Sometimes words with '–or' in American English

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.660
can be spelt in British English using '–our'.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:46.680
For example:

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.500
'What's your favorite color?'

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.840
changes to 'favourite' 'colour'.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.760
It doesn't matter whether you use

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:55.820
American English or British English spelling.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.620
Just make sure you use one or the other.

